Z||000000||Earl||11-17-2001||06:11 PM||||  I just worked out a deal for a crank, pistons and rods to stoke my duke to 3 liter And i need to know how much I can safely bore out the duke ..<BR>  and also I was wondering what version of desktop dyno is the best so I can get it ..||||reg||5
Z||000001||Earl||11-18-2001||01:35 AM||||  Any help at all? Anyone interested in doing the same thing?||||reg||1
Z||000002||kslish||11-18-2001||02:11 AM||||The less you bore the block the better.  The cylinder walls are pretty thin in the Duke.  Just do what it takes to clean the cylinder walls up and no more.  0.030" over is about the reasonable limit.<P>Where are you getting the crank, pistons and rods and who makes them?<P>Ken S.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Earl||11-18-2001||02:42 AM||||  I am getting them from a boat mech and they are made by GM for mercruiser its their 140 hp engine I will need almost 1/8" in the bore . Can I bore it that much? and still Have any walls left? If not would the truck engine be better?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Maniak84||11-18-2001||08:52 AM||||Nitrus, nitrus, nitrus!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Earl||11-18-2001||02:48 PM||||  I dont like NOS isn't kind to crankshafts and I aint going through that again .||||reg||1
Z||000006||FastFieros||11-18-2001||11:41 PM||||Earl,<P>I have built 52 2.5 duke motors and made 202 HP at the crank on at least 20 of them. You better have deep pockets playing with 2.5 motors and trying to make big numbers out of those things.<P>I raced Sprint cars for 9 years. I have built GSXR engines that cost 10k and 2.5 engines that cost 6k. Money buys speed!<P>Here is what I remember off the top of my head, and I am real sleepy right now. I will talk more by phone than typing. I hate to type.<P>The stock Fiero (r) motor will bore .080 and it getting THIN. The S-10 truck block will bore .120 and its getting THIN.<P>I used a crank that most have never heard of and its a OEM GM crank made in the early 70's for a NHRA racing class. Its called either the 355 or 352. Darn I dont remember, but guess what I have one still. It is a FORGED crank from Mexico for the NHRA class. I use to know the name of a wrecking yard in MO that had 4 of these things still.<P>You can make 171 Cu in and about 160HP on gas with a conbination of this crank and a rod from childs and abert, then a HOLDEN piston for some wierd GM motor down under. It been awhile since I had to remember this stuff. I just build V6s and V8s today.<P>BTW, the Mercrusier stuff is junk. We killed 4 engines with those cranks and they are for LOW RPM's. They come apart at 5800 and we turned 7600 with the forged cranks.<P>Hope this helps.<P>Loyde <A HREF="http://www.fastfieros.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fastfieros.com</A> ||||reg||14
Z||000007||ITB 2M4||11-19-2001||12:09 AM||||I have bored mine out .030, on a balanced and blueprinted motor for my '84 SE race car.  It's all stock pieces inside, and routinely sees 5400rpm.<P>You wouldn't happen to have the part number for the crank?<P>Hey, FastFieros, you know a guy named Alfred Whiteside?  When I lived in Corpus Christi, he used to run a tube frame Chevette in Super Gas. Then he moved to Dallas in '87 and opened up a shop up there, building race cars.<P>------------------<BR>SCCA ITB '84 Fiero 2M4||||reg||1
Z||000008||erikred||11-19-2001||03:29 PM||||Hey Fast Fiero, was this NHRA crank you are talking about installed on any vehicles, or was it a GM performance part?<P>Erik-||||reg||1
Z||000009||erikred||11-19-2001||03:34 PM||||Many racer are used to running high RPMs. When it comes to a street engine there is not as much need for high revs. It should be possible to build a close to 200hp Duke without going past 6000 RPM. I would think this would last decent for street or mild race use if not over reved too much.<P>Erik||||reg||1
